From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: report and fail an IDMAC bus error instead of dropping it

What. Turn on the two IDMAC error interrupts for every data request, both
directions, and handle them in the IRQ path:
- sunxi_mmc_start_dma() writes SMHC_IDIE = FERR_INT_ENB | DES_UNAVL_INT_ENB
  (| RX_INT_ENB on a read) right after the SDXC_IDMAC_SOFT_RESET, where the
  RX enable was written before. finalize still zeroes SMHC_IDIE.
- sunxi_mmc_irq(): on SMHC_IDST bit 2 or 4 with a data request open, one
  dev_err with SMHC_IDST raw and decoded (fatal-bus-error, desc-unavailable,
  card-error-sum, DMAC_ERR_STA[12:10]) and int_sum, then the request goes
  down the existing error path at once: data->error = -EIO (cmd->error stays
  -ETIMEDOUT like every other error here), the manual stop where
  sunxi_mmc_needs_manual_stop() asks for one, the data engine released,
  the core's block recovery takes over.
- Clearing is unchanged and is what the UM asks for: the handler already
  writes the SMHC_IDST value it read back (all used bits are R/W1C, AIS
  bit 9 has to be cleared whenever a bit that sets it is cleared -- it is
  in the same word), and release_data_engine()
  writes 0x337, the UM's own clear value (11.3.6.2 step 5).
The completion path is untouched: no new bit is looked at unless bit 2 or 4
is set, and neither is set on any IRQ logged so far (below).

Why. Before this, SMHC_IDIE held RX_INT_ENB on reads and nothing on
writes. A fatal bus error stops the IDMAC (it halts all bus
access, 11.3.8.27) and has no bit in SMHC_RINTSTS, so a read waits for an
RX_INT that cannot come, and a write for the end of a data phase whose
source has stopped (not measured which RINTSTS error, if any, follows) --
with no line in the log. If any other interrupt arrived meanwhile, the
handler wrote bit 2 back with the rest and cleared it unseen.

Register facts, A733 UM V1.00, ch. 11.3:
- SMHC_DMAC 0x0080 (11.3.8.25): bit 0 IDMAC_RST resets the DMA controller's
  own registers and clears itself. Which registers that covers is not stated;
  SMHC_IDIE is written after it on every request, so it does not matter.
- SMHC_IDST 0x0088 (11.3.8.27): bit 2 FATAL_BERR_INT R/W1C, bit 4
  DES_UNAVL_INT R/W1C (descriptor OWN bit 0, DES0[31] = 0), bit 5 ERR_FLAG_SUM
  R/W1C, bit 9 AIS = OR of 2/4/5 R/W1C, bits 12:10 DMAC_ERR_STA R, valid
  only while the fatal bus error bit is set, 001 host abort during transmission,
  010 during reception, others reserved.
- SMHC_IDIE 0x008C (11.3.8.28): bit 2 FERR_INT_ENB, bit 4
  DES_UNAVL_INT_ENB, bit 5 ERR_SUM_INT_ENB, bits 31:6 reserved.
- Silicon, the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-23 (SMHC0 read back on the board):
  SMHC0 IDIE reads 0x00000002 during a read, 0 between
  requests; IDST reads 0x0000A000 -- bits 15 and 13 are set although the UM
  marks 31:13 reserved, DMAC_ERR_STA 0. Nothing new is inferred from them.
- Logged IRQs so far (dyndbg "irq: ... idi", across three
  board logs): 861 x idi 00000002 and 228 x 00000000 --
  bit 4 never seen at the end of a normal descriptor chain.

Decisions.
- ERR_SUM_INT_ENB (bit 5) is NOT enabled. By the UM it is the OR of RINTSTS
  EBE/RTO/RCRC/SBE/DRTO/DCRC/RE (all also reported in RINTSTS), and this driver
  already unmasks and fails on those in SMHC_INTMASK. Enabling it adds no
  event, and treating it as an IDMAC failure would log a line per CRC error
  in the sample-delay sweep (`mmc-sunxi-tune-the-sample-delay-instead-of-running-it-at-zero`), which produces them by design. It is
  still decoded in the line when it happens to be set.
- There is no AIS enable. IDIE bits 2 and 4 are described as working only together
  with the Abnormal Interrupt Summary Enable, but the A733 IDIE marks
  everything above bit 5 reserved (the DesignWare IDINTEN this block
  resembles has NI/AI enables at bits 8/9). Reserved bits are not written.
  Whether bits 2/4 raise the IRQ line on their own is therefore the first
  thing the board has to show (below).
- Upstream names bit 4 SDXC_IDMAC_DESTINATION_INVALID (UM: DES_UNAVL_INT)
  and defines SDXC_IDMAC_HOST_ABORT_INTERRUPT as BIT(10), which on the A733
  is the low bit of the 3-bit DMAC_ERR_STA field. Neither is renamed here;
  the new SDXC_IDMAC_ERR_STA is the field as the UM defines it.

What the vendor does (allwinner-bsp drivers/mmc/sunxi-mmc.c). The same gap:
SMHC_IDIE = RECEIVE_INTERRUPT on reads only (sunxi-mmc.c:955-956), the IRQ
tests RX_INT alone and writes SMHC_IDST back (1286, 1322, 1346). It defines
FATAL_BUS_ERROR, DESTINATION_INVALID, CARD_ERROR_SUM and ABNORMAL_INTERRUPT_
SUM (sunxi-mmc.h:262-266) and uses none of them. What catches the hang
there is a periodic software timer (sunxi_timerout_handle, sunxi-mmc.c:270)
that dumps registers after SUNXI_TRANS_TIMEOUT -- a timeout that names no
cause. Nothing taken from it; this names the cause and fails the request
when it happens.

Verified: applies with --fuzz=0 in the full series on a fresh unpack;
drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.o builds at W=1 with 0 warnings (native arm64,
configs/a733_defconfig); checkpatch clean. Not on hardware.

Proof wanted (the 8 GiB board, a test kernel with its own version label, SD and eMMC):
1. Enables land: busybox devmem of SMHC_IDIE (0x0402008C SD, 0x0402208C
   eMMC) under a sustained read reads 0x00000016, under a sustained write
   0x00000014 (bit 2|4 held; 0x2/0x0 means they did not latch).
2. Normal path unchanged: zero "idmac error" lines over boot, a 256 MiB
   pattern write + verify on both media, sequential and 4k throughput
   within the spread of the default kernel.
3. The error path fires: a test-only kernel (not part of the series) that,
   on a debugfs trigger, clears DES0 OWN on the first descriptor of the next
   read must print "idmac error ... desc-unavailable", fail that request
   with -EIO, and the core's retry must complete it (read returns the right
   data, no hang). A fatal bus error has no safe stimulus we know of and
   stays unprovoked; its line is the same code path.
Falsifier: an "idmac error" line on a normal transfer (DU at the end of a
chain -> the DU enable is wrong here), IDIE reading back without bits 2/4,
or the step-3 stimulus hanging without a line (bits 2/4 do not raise the
IRQ without an AIS enable this IDIE does not have).

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
  * (C) Copyright 2017 Sootech SA
  */
 
+#include <linux/bitfield.h>
 #include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/clk/sunxi-ng.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
@@ -340,6 +341,18 @@
 #define SDXC_IDMAC_READ				(6 << 13)
 #define SDXC_IDMAC_WRITE			(7 << 13)
 #define SDXC_IDMAC_DESC_CLOSE			(8 << 13)
+/*
+ * The two IDMAC failures SMHC_RINTSTS cannot report, A733 UM 11.3.8.27/28:
+ * SMHC_IDST bit 2 FATAL_BERR_INT (the IDMAC halts all bus access)
+ * and bit 4 DES_UNAVL_INT (a descriptor with OWN = 0), enabled by SMHC_IDIE
+ * bits 2 FERR_INT_ENB and 4 DES_UNAVL_INT_ENB. Both R/W1C. Bit 5, the card
+ * error summary, is left out: it is the OR of RINTSTS errors this driver
+ * already unmasks and fails on.
+ */
+#define SDXC_IDMAC_ERROR_INT	(SDXC_IDMAC_FATAL_BUS_ERROR | \
+				 SDXC_IDMAC_DESTINATION_INVALID)
+/* SMHC_IDST[12:10] DMAC_ERR_STA, valid only with bit 2 set (11.3.8.27) */
+#define SDXC_IDMAC_ERR_STA	GENMASK(12, 10)
 
 /*
 * If the idma-des-size-bits of property is ie 13, bufsize bits are:
@@ -480,6 +493,7 @@
 	dma_addr_t	sg_dma;
 	void		*sg_cpu;
 	bool		wait_dma;
+	u32		idma_err;	/* SMHC_IDST of an IDMAC failure */
 
 	struct mmc_request *mrq;
 	struct mmc_request *manual_stop_mrq;
@@ -743,8 +757,15 @@
 
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_DMAC, SDXC_IDMAC_SOFT_RESET);
 
+	/*
+	 * Written after the soft reset on every request: the UM does not say
+	 * which "internal registers" IDMAC_RST clears, and finalize zeroes
+	 * SMHC_IDIE anyway. The error enables go on for both directions.
+	 */
+	rval = SDXC_IDMAC_ERROR_INT;
 	if (!(data->flags & MMC_DATA_WRITE))
-		mmc_writel(host, REG_IDIE, SDXC_IDMAC_RECEIVE_INTERRUPT);
+		rval |= SDXC_IDMAC_RECEIVE_INTERRUPT;
+	mmc_writel(host, REG_IDIE, rval);
 
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_DMAC,
 		   SDXC_IDMAC_FIX_BURST | SDXC_IDMAC_IDMA_ON);
@@ -1081,12 +1102,13 @@
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_IMASK, host->sdio_imask);
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_IDIE, 0);
 
-	if (host->int_sum & sunxi_mmc_error_bits(host, mrq)) {
+	if ((host->int_sum & sunxi_mmc_error_bits(host, mrq)) ||
+	    host->idma_err) {
 		sunxi_mmc_dump_errinfo(host);
 		mrq->cmd->error = -ETIMEDOUT;
 
 		if (data) {
-			data->error = -ETIMEDOUT;
+			data->error = host->idma_err ? -EIO : -ETIMEDOUT;
 			if (sunxi_mmc_needs_manual_stop(mrq))
 				host->manual_stop_mrq = mrq;
 		}
@@ -1141,6 +1163,7 @@
 
 	host->mrq = NULL;
 	host->int_sum = 0;
+	host->idma_err = 0;
 	host->wait_dma = false;
 
 	return host->manual_stop_mrq ? IRQ_WAKE_THREAD : IRQ_HANDLED;
@@ -1436,13 +1459,37 @@
 
 		host->int_sum |= msk_int;
 
+		/*
+		 * The IDMAC stopped on a bus error or an unowned descriptor.
+		 * Neither reaches SMHC_RINTSTS, so without this the data never
+		 * completes and nothing says why. Fail the data through the
+		 * error path below; the status is cleared with the rest.
+		 */
+		if (mrq->data && (idma_int & SDXC_IDMAC_ERROR_INT) &&
+		    !host->idma_err) {
+			host->idma_err = idma_int;
+			dev_err(mmc_dev(host->mmc),
+				"cmd %d %s: idmac error, IDST %08x:%s%s%s DMAC_ERR_STA %lu, int_sum %08x\n",
+				mrq->cmd->opcode,
+				mrq->data->flags & MMC_DATA_WRITE ? "WR" : "RD",
+				idma_int,
+				idma_int & SDXC_IDMAC_FATAL_BUS_ERROR ?
+					" fatal-bus-error" : "",
+				idma_int & SDXC_IDMAC_DESTINATION_INVALID ?
+					" desc-unavailable" : "",
+				idma_int & SDXC_IDMAC_CARD_ERROR_SUM ?
+					" card-error-sum" : "",
+				FIELD_GET(SDXC_IDMAC_ERR_STA, idma_int),
+				host->int_sum);
+		}
+
 		/* Wait for COMMAND_DONE on RESPONSE_TIMEOUT before finalize */
 		if ((host->int_sum & SDXC_RESP_TIMEOUT) &&
 				!(host->int_sum & SDXC_COMMAND_DONE))
 			mmc_writel(host, REG_IMASK,
 				   host->sdio_imask | SDXC_COMMAND_DONE);
 		/* Don't wait for dma on error */
-		else if (host->int_sum & err_bits)
+		else if ((host->int_sum & err_bits) || host->idma_err)
 			finalize = true;
 		else if (host->busy_wait) {
 			/*
